{"item_id": "feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/black-diamond-distance-15-backpack-review", "version_id": "72a94336aee944d2865b19aa22833a0909ed2257f9513c0bece6e9d6948f3840", "decision": "accept", "reason": "In scope under the Relevance rule as a rucking/endurance pack, and the backcountry-hunting-source exception does not apply because the review is hands-on mountain running and hiking gear. The author reports roughly 200 miles of use with specific field observations on load stability, pocket access, durability, and fit, and notes the affiliate model per the Commercial incentives rule. Only one publisher is involved, so the evidence is single-source and should be attributed, with the sponsor-free aside that the Pemi and Fastpack comparisons are the publisher's own. Best supports piece 03 (what readers should test: fit sizing and load stability when heavy), with piece 02 as a secondary fit for the manufacturer's durability claim tested in the field.", "piece_ids": ["03", "02"]}
{"item_id": "feed:https://www.treelinereview.com/gearreviews/camping-outdoor-blankets", "version_id": "4cee453e4397415960c54f3e35b7b1cac2b511cd28774f12f81d4d6a7c5f0427", "decision": "reject", "reason": "Under the Relevance rule, camping blankets and picnic blankets are not in the listed gear categories, so the item is out of scope for a rucking and spotting-gear publication. Although the publication is an affiliate-model site with its own water tests, nothing in the blanket content bears on Mammoth conditions, so the Making the decision rule points to rejection rather than deferral.", "piece_ids": []}
